import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About & Philosophy" description="Learn about A11y Test Kitchen's non-simulator framing and role-based accessibility teaching philosophy.">
  <article class="about-article">
    <header class="about-header">
      <h1 class="about-title">About A11y Test Kitchen</h1>
      <p class="about-lead">
        An interactive exhibit designed to judge the interface, never the person.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>The Framing Philosophy</h2>
      <p>
        Simulators such as blur goggles, shaky cursors, or input disablers are widely criticized by disabled advocates. A short filter cannot replicate anyone's lived experience, and it often creates pity instead of structural understanding.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A11y Test Kitchen rejects disability simulation.</strong> We frame every demonstration around interface barriers and code fixes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>We say <em>“broken interface,” “barrier,”</em> and <em>“the fix.”</em></li>
        <li>We never say <em>“feel what disability feels like”</em> or <em>“experience what it's like to be blind.”</em></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Three Audience Perspectives</h2>
      <p>
        Every station includes explanations tailored for three distinct roles:
      </p>
      <dl class="roles-list">
        <dt><strong>Advocate Mode:</strong></dt>
        <dd>For managers, executives, and clients. Focuses on human and organizational impact, who is affected most, and clear decision takeaways with zero source code.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Builder Mode:</strong></dt>
        <dd>For developers and architects. Details exact WCAG success criteria, concrete implementation changes, practical snippets, and test instructions.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Presenter Mode:</strong></dt>
        <dd>For live demonstrators. Provides a short, timed run sheet and three closing lines.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Site Accessibility Floor</h2>
      <p>
        Even though stations demonstrate broken interaction patterns, the host website follows a documented safety floor:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Every page starts with a skip link and keeps a native keyboard escape path.</li>
        <li>Focus rings are authored for at least 3:1 contrast against their backgrounds.</li>
        <li>Layouts use fluid rules; the release checklist requires reflow checks at documented zoom and viewport sizes.</li>
        <li><code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> removes authored transitions and animations.</li>
        <li>State indicators always combine <strong>Color + Word + Shape</strong> (Plum Diamond for Barrier, Slate Blue Circle for Fixed).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Claims and limits</h2>
      <p>
        Barrier panels intentionally demonstrate narrow failures, so this site does not make a blanket WCAG conformance claim. Fixed examples are reviewed against named criteria, and the release checklist combines automation with keyboard, zoom, browser, and real assistive-technology checks. One passing tool does not prove full access.
      </p>
    </section>

